  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "Born in Paris, de Lasteyrie began at BNP and the Louis Dreyfus group before assisting Bernard Arnault’s acquisition of Boussac. He subsequently pursued concentrated minority stakes through Valauret, targeting companies where he believed management changes could unlock value. His move to Belgium produced a landmark European Court of Justice judgment that found France’s immediate tax on unrealised gains restricted freedom of establishment.\n\nThe most public battle of de Lasteyrie’s career followed heavy losses on Rhodia shares. He demanded board changes and pursued civil and criminal complaints alleging misleading accounts and concealed debt. The financier continued working with investigators until the day before his sudden death from a heart attack in Paris. His heirs carried forward related legal claims after his death.",
    "html": "<p>Born in Paris, de Lasteyrie began at BNP and the Louis Dreyfus group before assisting Bernard Arnault’s acquisition of Boussac. He subsequently pursued concentrated minority stakes through Valauret, targeting companies where he believed management changes could unlock value. His move to Belgium produced a landmark European Court of Justice judgment that found France’s immediate tax on unrealised gains restricted freedom of establishment.</p>\r\n<p>The most public battle of de Lasteyrie’s career followed heavy losses on Rhodia shares. He demanded board changes and pursued civil and criminal complaints alleging misleading accounts and concealed debt. The financier continued working with investigators until the day before his sudden death from a heart attack in Paris. His heirs carried forward related legal claims after his death.</p>\r\n",
